A(('p', '7.3 Two readings tested. Two readings are common, and each can be set beside the words. (a) The “servant” of Isaiah 41–53 is one figure in every passage. The text names Israel and Jacob as the servant in the verses of the first table, introduces a servant without a name in 42:1 and 52:13, and says “Thou art my servant, O Israel” in 49:3 beside a work “to bring Jacob again to him” in 49:5; it does not state that these are one figure or several. The study records the verses and does not decide. (b) The New Testament quotations settle whom each Isaiah passage names. Matthew 12:18 quotes Isaiah 42:1 with “my servant,” and Acts 8:35 states that Philip preached “Jesus” from Isaiah 53; each statement is made in its own chapter. The study reports what each says and does not turn one quotation into a general conclusion for the others.'))

A(('h1', 'Conclusion'))
A(('p', 'Isaiah 41–53 uses “my servant” in more than one kind of sentence. In 41:8–9, 44:1, 44:21, 45:4 and 48:20 the servant is Israel or Jacob. In 42:1–9 and 52:13–53:12 the LORD speaks of “my servant” without a name, and describes what he shall do and what he suffers. In 49:1–7 and 50:4–10 a speaker reports his call and his words. The New Testament quotes these chapters in Matthew, Luke, John, Acts, Romans and 1 Peter, each in its own setting. The study has recorded what each passage states, whom it addresses and where it stops.'))

A(('h1', 'Study Summary'))
A(('bul', [
    'Isaiah 41:8–9, 44:1–2, 44:21, 45:4 and 48:20 name Israel and Jacob as “my servant,” with the verbs “chosen,” “formed,” “not … forgotten” and “redeemed.”',
    'Isaiah 42:1–9 introduces “my servant” without a name, states what he shall not do and shall do, and addresses “thee” with “a covenant of the people” and “a light of the Gentiles.”',
    'Isaiah 49:1–7 reports “Thou art my servant, O Israel” beside a work “to bring Jacob again”; Isaiah 50:4–10 records a speaker’s words without naming him.',
    'Isaiah 52:13–53:12 moves from “my servant” exalted to a servant afflicted, with “we” and “our” in 53:1–6 and “my righteous servant” in 53:11.',
    'The New Testament quotations stand in Matthew 8 and 12, Luke 22, John 12, Acts 8, Romans 10 and 15 and 1 Peter 2, each read in its own chapter.',
]))

A(('h1', 'Key Distinctions to Retain'))
A(('bul', [
    'The word “servant” is used in several kinds of sentence in Isaiah 41–53: a named Israel or Jacob, an unnamed servant, and a speaker. Each is read where it stands.',
    'A name given in one passage is not carried into another unless the passage itself does so; Isaiah 49:3 names Israel, and Isaiah 52:13 names no one.',
    'Each New Testament quotation is read in its own chapter and none answers another; where a verse states what was preached or fulfilled, the study records it and adds nothing.',
]))

